AVM: ANGLE finds its own libraries beside itself on macOS

ANGLE's libEGL opens libGLESv2 as GLESv2.framework/Versions/Current/GLESv2
on macOS, a framework path relative to the current folder, which UTM's
app makes work. AVM ships ANGLE as libEGL.dylib and libGLESv2.dylib, in
the sysroot's lib folder and in AVM.app's Resources, so QEMU with 3D
graphics stopped at start: "Error loading EGL entry points:
dlopen(GLESv2.framework/Versions/Current/GLESv2) failed" (2026-10-27).
This tries lib<name>.dylib in the loading library's own folder first,
then the framework path as before.

A git clone (UTM's WebKit, for ANGLE): UTM's build patches only
downloaded sources, so Tools/build-sysroot.sh applies this just before
ANGLE is built.

--- a/Source/ThirdParty/ANGLE/src/common/system_utils.cpp
+++ b/Source/ThirdParty/ANGLE/src/common/system_utils.cpp
@@ -219,6 +219,13 @@
                                    std::string *errorOut)
 {
 #if defined(ANGLE_PLATFORM_MACOS)
+    // AVM: lib<name>.dylib beside this library first.
+    std::string besideName = std::string("lib") + libraryName + ".dylib";
+    if (void *beside = OpenSystemLibraryWithExtensionAndGetError(besideName.c_str(),
+                                                                 SearchType::ModuleDir, nullptr))
+    {
+        return beside;
+    }
     std::string frameworkPath = std::string(libraryName) + ".framework/Versions/Current/" + std::string(libraryName);
     return OpenSystemLibraryWithExtensionAndGetError(frameworkPath.c_str(), SearchType::SystemDir, errorOut);
 #elif defined(ANGLE_PLATFORM_IOS_FAMILY)
